The '''2008 NBA Draft''' was held on June 26, 2008, at the [[The Theater at Madison Square Garden|Washington Mutual Theatre]] at [[Madison Square Garden]] in New York City, New York. In this [[NBA draft|draft]], [[National Basketball Association]] (NBA) teams took turns selecting amateur college [[basketball]] players and other first-time [[Eligibility for the NBA Draft|eligible players]], including international players from non-North American professional leagues. According to the NBA, 44 players, 39 collegiate players and five international players, filed as early-entry candidates for the 2008 NBA Draft.<ref>{{Cite web |date=June 17, 2008 |title=Final Draft Early Entry Candidate List |url=http://www.nba.com/news/draft_list_080617.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090618054147/http://www.nba.com/news/draft_list_080617.html |archive-date=June 18, 2009 |access-date=May 3, 2009 |website=NBA.com |publisher=Turner Sports Interactive, Inc}}</ref> These numbers do not include players who are [[Eligibility for the NBA Draft#Automatic eligibility|automatically eligible]] for the draft. The [[Chicago Bulls]], who had a 1.7 percent probability of obtaining the first selection, won the [[NBA draft lottery]] on May 22.<ref>{{Cite web |date=May 22, 2008 |title=Chicago Bulls Win No. 1 Pick in NBA Draft Lottery |url=http://www.nba.com/nba_news/bulls_toppick_080520.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170620073456/http://www.nba.com/nba_news/bulls_toppick_080520.html |archive-date=June 20, 2017 |access-date=March 29, 2009 |website=NBA.com |publisher=Turner Sports Interactive, Inc}}</ref><ref name="ESPN">{{Cite web |date=May 21, 2008 |title=Bulls to pick first in draft; Heat, Wolves round out Top 3 |url=https://www.espn.com/nba/draft2008/news/story?id=3405648 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081017164510/http://sports.espn.go.com/nba/draft2008/news/story?id=3405648 |archive-date=October 17, 2008 |access-date=March 29, 2009 |website=ESPN.com}}</ref> The Bulls' winning of the lottery was the second-largest upset in NBA Draft Lottery history behind the [[Orlando Magic]], who won it in [[1993 NBA draft|1993]] with just a 1.5% chance.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Year-by-Year Lottery Probabilities |url=http://www.nba.com/history/lottery_probabilities.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080724134422/http://www.nba.com/history/lottery_probabilities.html |archive-date=July 24, 2008 |access-date=July 3, 2008 |website=NBA.com |publisher=Turner Sports Interactive, Inc}}</ref> The [[Miami Heat]] and the [[Minnesota Timberwolves]] obtained the second and third picks respectively.<ref name="ESPN" />

For the first time in draft history the first three draft picks were all freshmen.<ref name="ESPNroundup">{{Cite web |title=Bulls go for Rose over Beasley in NBA draft; Mukendi Love swap places |url=https://www.espn.com/nba/draft2008/news/story?id=3462594 |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080628043609/http://sports.espn.go.com/nba/draft2008/news/story?id=3462594 |archive-date=June 28, 2008 |access-date=May 16, 2008 |website=ESPN.com}}</ref> The Chicago Bulls used the [[List of first overall NBA draft picks|first overall pick]] to draft Chicago native [[Derrick Rose]] from the [[University of Memphis]], who later went on to win the [[NBA Rookie of the Year Award]], making him the first player to be drafted first overall and to win Rookie of the Year since [[LeBron James]] in 2003, and also became the youngest player to win the NBA Most Valuable Player Award in 2011 at age 22.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Rookie of the Year Award |url=http://www.nba.com/history/awards_rookieofyear.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090428174922/http://www.nba.com/history/awards_rookieofyear.html |archive-date=April 28, 2009 |access-date=May 3, 2009 |website=NBA.com |publisher=Turner Sports Interactive, Inc}}</ref> The Miami Heat used the second pick to draft [[Michael Beasley]] from [[Kansas State University]], and the Minnesota Timberwolves used the third pick to draft [[O. J. Mayo]] from [[The University of Southern California]].<ref name="Basketball Reference">{{Cite web |title=2008 NBA Draft |url=https://www.basketball-reference.com/draft/NBA_2008.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080628043609/http://sports.espn.go.com/nba/draft2008/news/story?id=3462594 |archive-date=June 28, 2008 |access-date=April 18, 2020 |website=basketball-reference.com}}</ref> The Seattle SuperSonics used their 4th overall pick to draft [[Russell Westbrook]] from [[University of California, Los Angeles|The University of California, Los Angeles]], who would go on to win the 2017 NBA MVP award and is widely considered the best player in this draft. With five players taken in the draft, the [[University of Kansas]] tied [[University of Connecticut]] ([[2006 NBA draft|2006]]) and [[University of Florida]] ([[2007 NBA draft|2007]]) for the record with the most players selected in the first two rounds of an NBA draft until the [[University of Kentucky]] ([[2012 NBA draft|2012]]) broke the record with six players drafted.<ref name="ESPNroundup" /> Another record was set when twelve freshmen were drafted, ten of whom were drafted in the first round.<ref name="SIdraft">{{Cite web |last=Thomsen |first=Ian |date=June 27, 2008 |title=After top two, lack of locks spreads uncertainty in volatile draft |url=http://sportsillustrated.cnn.com/2008/writers/ian_thomsen/06/27/nba.draft2/index.html?eref=T1 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080630175311/http://sportsillustrated.cnn.com/2008/writers/ian_thomsen/06/27/nba.draft2/index.html?eref=T1 |archive-date=June 30, 2008 |access-date=June 28, 2008 |website=SI.com |publisher=Time Inc}}</ref> Of the players drafted, 29 are [[Basketball position#Frontcourt (forwards and center)|forward]]s, 19 are [[Basketball position#Backcourt (guards)|guard]]s, and 12 are [[Center (basketball)|center]]s.

The 2008 NBA Draft was the final time that the [[Seattle SuperSonics]] made an NBA Draft appearance, as well as the final time that the Sonics appeared in official media publications. In early July, the franchise [[Seattle SuperSonics relocation to Oklahoma City|relocated]] to [[Oklahoma City, Oklahoma]], and was renamed the [[Oklahoma City Thunder]]. The Thunder made their first NBA Draft appearance in [[2009 NBA draft|2009]].<ref name="thunder">{{Cite web |last=Pian Chan |first=Sharon |author-link=Sharon Chan (journalist) |date=July 2, 2008 |title=Sonics, city reach settlement |url=http://seattletimes.nwsource.com/html/localnews/2008030229_sonitrial02.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080703210555/http://seattletimes.nwsource.com/html/localnews/2008030229_sonitrial02.html |archive-date=July 3, 2008 |access-date=July 2, 2008 |website=Seattle Times}}</ref> This draft also marked the first time that an [[NBA Development League|NBA D-League]] player was drafted.<ref>{{Cite web |date=June 26, 2008 |title=Idaho's Mike Taylor Becomes First D-League Player Drafted By An NBA Team |url=http://www.nba.com/dleague/news/taylor_080627.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120113105925/http://www.nba.com/dleague/news/taylor_080627.html |archive-date=January 13, 2012 |access-date=March 16, 2012 |website=NBA.com |publisher=Turner Sports Interactive, Inc}}</ref> As of 2025, there are six active players from this draft who are still playing in the NBA: [[Russell Westbrook]], [[Kevin Love]], [[Eric Gordon]], [[Brook Lopez]], [[Nicolas Batum]] and [[DeAndre Jordan]].

